  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through Monday with worsening drought \nconditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nDry conditions a virtual certainty today, Sunday, and Monday. \nHigh pressure will be centered to our south, bringing breezy \ndaytime winds across the region. The high pressure will continue\nto pipe warm air into the region from the desert SW. Thus a \nwarming trend will persist throughout the rest of the week with \ntemps reaching into the 60s for the south and upper 50s for the \nnorth. \n\nFor those venturing outside this (Halloween) evening, there is\nhigh confidence in no precipitation, but it will be cool, with\ntemperatures at sunset in the mid to upper 30s north, and low\n40s Bangor/Downeast. By 9pm, it will fall to the low 30s north \nand mid to upper 30s Bangor/Downeast. Winds are expected to calm.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances return Monday night and remain \nthrough the rest of the week, but very low confidence on whether\nor not there will be notable drought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nBy Monday night into Tuesday, an occluded low pressure system \nnorth of the Great Lakes is expected to stretch a warm front \ninto the north. Ensemble model trends are still uncertain with \nthe track and timing, which push rain chances into the 15 to 30 \npercent chance for the north. If this precipitation occurs \nMonday night/Tuesday, it would likely be a quarter inch or less.\n\nHeading into Tuesday night, Wednesday, and beyond, \nforecast confidence drops greatly. There is very \nlow confidence in whether or not there will be any rain. Some \nensembles/models have decent rain, while others have none. \nHeading toward Thursday night/Friday, there is a little better \nsignal for a stronger low pressure or cold front and a decent \nrain event. That said, confidence is still very low.",
